Horizontal Scaling vs Vertical Scaling are two ways to increase the capacity of a system (server, database, application).

1️⃣ Vertical Scaling (Scale Up / Down)

Meaning: Increase the power of a single machine.

Examples:

Add more RAM

Add more CPU

Add faster SSD

📌 Example
Your server has:

4 CPU

8 GB RAM

You upgrade it to:

16 CPU

64 GB RAM

➡️ This is Vertical Scaling.

Diagram

Before
[ Server ]
CPU: 4
RAM: 8GB

After
[ Bigger Server ]
CPU: 16
RAM: 64GB

✅ Advantages

Easy to implement

No major architecture changes

❌ Disadvantages

Hardware limit exists

Expensive

Single point of failure

2️⃣ Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out / In)

Meaning: Add more machines (servers) instead of upgrading one.

Example:
Instead of 1 server:

[ Server 1 ]

You add more servers:

[ Server 1 ]   [ Server 2 ]   [ Server 3 ]
        \        |        /
        [ Load Balancer ]

➡️ Traffic is distributed across servers.

📌 Real example

Netflix

Amazon

Google

These companies use thousands of servers (horizontal scaling).

✅ Advantages

Almost unlimited scaling

High availability

Fault tolerant

❌ Disadvantages

Complex architecture

Requires load balancer

3️⃣ Simple Interview Answer (Best)

Vertical Scaling: Increase resources of a single server (CPU, RAM).

Horizontal Scaling: Increase the number of servers and distribute load.

4️⃣ Real Example (Microservices / Spring Boot)

Suppose your Spring Boot application handles 1000 users.

Vertical

Upgrade server:

8GB RAM → 32GB RAM
Horizontal

Run multiple instances:

Spring Boot Instance 1
Spring Boot Instance 2
Spring Boot Instance 3
        ↓
    Load Balancer

✅ Interview Tip

Most modern systems prefer Horizontal Scaling because it supports cloud architecture and microservices.